#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dce1c69-c79d-541a-b479-8fc127352c2a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "e6a96fc8-6ce5-5f47-8c20-709754ed7bc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5811" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the graph of $y =2x^3- 6 x^2 +8?$\n\n![](images/3e/3e01f41691c4f157964157f30678f98977ac8bdf9908d89bd3e9a8817a6e2b91.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)=2x^3 -6 x^2 + 8\n$$\n\nThe degree ($n=3$) is odd and the leading coefficient ($a_3 = 2$) is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)$ approaches $-\\infty$ as as $x$ approaches $-\\infty.$\n\n- $P(x)$ approaches $\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $\\infty.$\n\nOnly the graph $\\textbf{C}$ satisfies these conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "7098a9a5-23ea-5496-a492-191c9c1a0690" :question/math-academy-id "e-5815" :question/problem "![](images/84/84c37b1692f6807873d924fad0d3591298c2b46b780797ed258dcf32d2ff0d8a.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials is shown in the diagram above?\n\n- $-2 x^4 + 6 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 6 x + 4$\n\n- $-2 x^5 + 6 x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x + 1$\n\n- $3 x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x - 4$\n\n- $- x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x - 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty.$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nSo, $P(x)$ is neither II nor III.\n\nLastly, note that the $y$-intercept is $4,$ which means that $P(0)$ must be equal to $4.$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)$ is given by polynomial I."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "fdba6918-c9be-5a77-b79c-4a5cf39bf6e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5817" :question/problem "The graph of a polynomial $Q(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6729ee13a704ce630586cd4cbdde5c67e55c03f65f1840b2ca1f2b6c38186f2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The coefficient of the leading term is positive.\n\n- The degree of $Q(x)$ is odd.\n\n- The constant term is negative." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $Q(x)\\to\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty,$ and\n\n- $Q(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $Q(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $Q(x)$ is negative.\n\nSo I is false, and II is true.\n\nLastly, note that the $y$-intercept is $0,$ which means that $Q(0)$ must be equal to $0,$ and therefore the constant term must be equal to $0.$ So, III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6faebac8-ebda-5f28-9ae5-f47ea62ba4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461665} {:answer/id #uuid "89f5755d-41a2-5951-8169-455f39e871ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f5755d-4